---
// The vertical rhythm of the whole landing page is defined here and nowhere
// else. Four steps, and every vertical margin on the page is one of them:
//
//   py-14   (3.5rem)  a band's own top and bottom padding, identical for every
//                     section and for the footer. Two bands therefore sit 7rem
//                     apart across their hairline while two blocks inside one
//                     band sit 5rem apart, so a band can never read as a
//                     continuation of the one above it.
//   mt-10   (2.5rem)  a section head to its content, and one block to the next
//                     block. Where a hairline separates two blocks it is
//                     centred in that space: `mt-10 border-t border-rule
//                     pt-10`. The gaps of a grid of blocks use the same step:
//                     `gap-10`.
//   mt-5    (1.25rem) a subsection head to the block it opens; a trailing
//                     pointer link below the block it points out of; and the
//                     padding of a hairline-separated row inside a list, as
//                     `border-t border-rule py-5` or `space-y-5` + `pt-5`,
//                     which puts 1.25rem on each side of the row's rule and so
//                     leaves 2.5rem between two rows' content.
//   mt-3    (0.75rem) a block to the note, caption or legend that explains it.
//
// A subsection is therefore always the same three gaps:
//
//   (mt-10 [hairline] pt-10) SubHead -> mt-5 block -> mt-3 note
//
// A head has twice the air above it that it has below, which is what makes it
// read as opening its block rather than labelling the line under it.
//
// Inside a block, rows are spaced with gap-2 (a key/value or data row) or
// space-y-3 (a list of prose items). Those are intra-block and not part of the
// scale above.
//
// Prose is capped at max-w-[68ch] rather than at a rem width, so the measure is
// the same count of characters whether the paragraph is text-sm or text-xs, and
// a transcript column cannot drag it wider: every grid that holds one declares
// grid-cols-1 and minmax(0,1fr) tracks, and the transcript scrolls inside it.
//
// A grid of blocks also carries items-baseline, so two blocks side by side line
// up on their first baseline rather than on their box tops.
//
// The head below is the strongest thing in its band: a two-digit mono index and
// a text-lg/text-xl title. SubHead.astro is one step quieter and is the only
// other heading pattern on the page.
//
// Both are monospaced while the prose under them is not, which is the split
// docs.css states: mono for headings, labels, identifiers, commands and numeric
// readouts, sans for running prose.
interface Props {
  index: string;
  title: string;
  id: string;
}

const { index, title, id } = Astro.props;
---

<section id={id} class="border-t border-rule">
  <div
    class="mx-auto box-border w-full max-w-6xl px-6 py-14"
    data-reveal
  >
    <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3 font-mono">
      <span class="text-xs text-gray-400" aria-hidden="true">{index}</span>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-medium tracking-tight text-gray-100 sm:text-xl">
        {title}
      </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-10">
      <slot />
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
